WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.560
Bounds-safe C depends on carrying size information correctly.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:14.580
A pointer alone usually does not tell a function how many elements are valid, how much capacity remains, or whether the data is meant to be a byte region, an element array, or a C string.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:17.440
The size contract must travel with the pointer.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:22.220
When a function reads from a buffer, it needs to know how many elements or bytes are valid to read.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.520
When a function writes to a buffer, it needs to know the destination capacity.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:31.220
When a function appends, it needs both the current count and total capacity.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.840
These are different facts, and merging them into one vague parameter makes review harder.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.300
Count and capacity should stay separate.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.640
Count describes how much meaningful data is currently present.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.500
Capacity describes how much storage is available.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.000
A buffer can have capacity for one thousand bytes while currently holding only a short message.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:57.340
Append logic needs both numbers, and failure behavior should update neither unless the operation succeeds in a documented way.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:58.600
Units matter.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:05.540
A length in bytes is not the same as a count of int elements, structure elements, or characters in a multibyte encoding.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.480
Function names, parameter names, and documentation should make the unit clear.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.500
A code review should not require guessing whether a value named size means bytes, elements, capacity, or current count.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.960
Avoid APIs that accept a raw pointer but not a valid length when the function needs bounds knowledge.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.420
For simple internal helpers, passing pointer plus count may be enough.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:37.360
For repeated patterns, a small structured buffer type can keep pointer, count, and capacity together so they are updated consistently and reviewed as one object.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.620
Good size propagation also supports maintainability.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:49.080
When the next developer changes a parser, extends a record, or moves a copy into a helper function, the needed bounds information should already be present.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:55.220
The safest API is often the one that makes the correct data flow natural and the incorrect data flow awkward.
